#: src/getting-started.md:831
msgid "Diving into the controller implementation"
msgstr ""

#: src/getting-started.md:833
msgid "Setting manager to watching resources"
msgstr ""

#: src/getting-started.md:835
msgid ""
"The whole idea is to be Watching the resources that matter for the "
"controller. When a resource that the controller is interested in changes, "
"the Watch triggers the controller's reconciliation loop, ensuring that the "
"actual state of the resource matches the desired state as defined in the "
"controller's logic."
msgstr ""

#: src/getting-started.md:840
msgid ""
"Notice how you configure the Manager to monitor events such as the creation, "
"update, or deletion of a Custom Resource (CR) of the Memcached kind, as well "
"as any changes to the Deployment that the controller manages and owns:"
msgstr ""

#: src/getting-started.md:844
msgid ""
"// SetupWithManager sets up the controller with the Manager.\n"
"// The Deployment is also watched to ensure its\n"
"// desired state in the cluster.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/getting-started.md:849
msgid ""
"// Watch the Memcached Custom Resource and trigger reconciliation whenever "
"it\n"
"\t\t//when you create, update, or delete it\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/getting-started.md:852
msgid ""
"// Watch the Deployment managed by the Memcached controller. If any changes "
"occur to the Deployment\n"
"        // owned and managed by this controller, it triggers reconciliation, "
"ensuring that the cluster\n"
"        // state aligns with the desired state.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/getting-started.md:860
msgid "But, how does the manager know which resources are owned by it?"
msgstr ""

#: src/getting-started.md:862
msgid ""
"The Controller should not watch any Deployment on the cluster and trigger "
"the reconciliation loop. Instead, trigger reconciliation only when the "
"specific Deployment running the Memcached instance is changed. For example, "
"if someone accidentally deletes the Deployment or changes the number of "
"replicas, trigger the reconciliation to ensure that it returns to the "
"desired state."
msgstr ""

#: src/getting-started.md:868
msgid ""
"The Manager knows which Deployment to observe because you set the `ownerRef` "
"(Owner Reference):"
msgstr ""

#: src/getting-started.md:880
msgid ""
"The ownerRef is crucial not only for allowing the controller to observe "
"changes on the specific resource but also because, if you delete the "
"Memcached Custom Resource (CR) from the cluster, all resources owned by it "
"are automatically deleted as well, in a cascading event."
msgstr ""

#: src/getting-started.md:884
msgid ""
"This ensures that when you remove the parent resource (Memcached CR), "
"Kubernetes also removes all associated resources (like Deployments, "
"Services, etc.) are also cleaned up, maintaining a tidy and consistent "
"cluster state."
msgstr ""

#: src/getting-started.md:888
msgid ""
"For more information, see the Kubernetes documentation on [Owners and "
"Dependents](https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/overview/working-with-objects/owners-dependents/)."
msgstr ""

#: src/getting-started.md:892
msgid "Granting permissions"
msgstr ""

#: src/getting-started.md:894
msgid ""
"It's important to ensure that the Controller has the necessary permissions "
"(i.e., to create, get, update, and list) the resources it manages."
msgstr ""

#: src/getting-started.md:897
msgid ""
"You configure the [RBAC "
"permissions](https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/access-authn-authz/rbac/) "
"via [RBAC markers](./reference/markers/rbac.md), which "
"[controller-gen](./reference/controller-gen.md) uses to generate and update "
"the manifest files in `config/rbac/`. You can find these markers (and should "
"define them) on the `Reconcile()` method of each controller, see how the "
"example implements them:"
msgstr ""

#: src/getting-started.md:910
msgid ""
"After making changes to the controller, run the make manifests command. This "
"will prompt [controller-gen](./reference/controller-gen.md) to refresh the "
"files located under `config/rbac`."
msgstr ""

