#: src/cronjob-tutorial/gvks.md:1
msgid "Groups and versions and kinds, oh my!"
msgstr ""

#: src/cronjob-tutorial/gvks.md:3
msgid "Before getting started with the API, talk about terminology a bit."
msgstr ""

#: src/cronjob-tutorial/gvks.md:6
msgid ""
"When discussing APIs in Kubernetes, 4 terms are often used: _groups_, "
"_versions_, _kinds_, and _resources_."
msgstr ""

#: src/cronjob-tutorial/gvks.md:9
msgid "Groups and versions"
msgstr ""

#: src/cronjob-tutorial/gvks.md:11
msgid ""
"An _API Group_ in Kubernetes is simply a collection of related "
"functionality.  Each group has one or more _versions_, which, as the name "
"suggests, allow you to change how an API works over time."
msgstr ""

#: src/cronjob-tutorial/gvks.md:15
msgid "Kinds and resources"
msgstr ""

#: src/cronjob-tutorial/gvks.md:17
msgid ""
"Each API group-version contains one or more API types, called _Kinds_.  "
"While a Kind may change forms between versions, each form must be able to "
"store all the data of the other forms, somehow (you can store the data in "
"fields, or in annotations).  This means that using an older API version will "
"not cause newer data to be lost or corrupted.  See the [Kubernetes API "
"guidelines](https://git.k8s.io/community/contributors/devel/sig-architecture/api-conventions.md) "
"for more information."
msgstr ""

#: src/cronjob-tutorial/gvks.md:26
msgid ""
"You'll also hear mention of _resources_ on occasion.  A resource is simply a "
"use of a Kind in the API.  Often, there is a one-to-one mapping between "
"Kinds and resources.  For instance, the `pods` resource corresponds to the "
"`Pod` Kind.  However, sometimes, the same Kind may be returned by multiple "
"resources.  For instance, the `Scale` Kind is returned by all scale "
"subresources, like `deployments/scale` or `replicasets/scale`.  This is what "
"allows the Kubernetes HorizontalPodAutoscaler to interact with different "
"resources.  With CRDs, however, each Kind will correspond to a single "
"resource."
msgstr ""

#: src/cronjob-tutorial/gvks.md:36
msgid ""
"Notice that resources are always lowercase, and by convention are the "
"lowercase form of the Kind."
msgstr ""

#: src/cronjob-tutorial/gvks.md:39
msgid "So, how does that correspond to Go?"
msgstr ""

#: src/cronjob-tutorial/gvks.md:41
msgid ""
"When referring to a kind in a particular group version, it is called a "
"_GroupVersionKind_, or GVK for short.  Same with resources and GVR. As "
"you'll see shortly, each GVK corresponds to a given root Go type in a "
"package."
msgstr ""

#: src/cronjob-tutorial/gvks.md:46
msgid "Now that the terminology is clear, _actually_ create the API!"
msgstr ""

#: src/cronjob-tutorial/gvks.md:49
msgid "So, how can you create an API?"
msgstr ""

#: src/cronjob-tutorial/gvks.md:51
msgid ""
"In the next section, [Adding a new API](../cronjob-tutorial/new-api.html), "
"check how the tool helps you create your APIs with the command `kubebuilder "
"create api`."
msgstr ""

#: src/cronjob-tutorial/gvks.md:54
msgid ""
"The goal of this command is to create a Custom Resource (CR) and Custom "
"Resource Definition (CRD) for your Kind(s). To check it further see; [Extend "
"the Kubernetes API with "
"CustomResourceDefinitions](https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/extend-kubernetes/custom-resources/custom-resource-definitions/)."
msgstr ""

#: src/cronjob-tutorial/gvks.md:56
msgid "But, why create APIs at all?"
msgstr ""

#: src/cronjob-tutorial/gvks.md:58
msgid ""
"New APIs are how you teach Kubernetes about your custom objects. "
"Controller-gen uses the Go structs to generate a CRD which includes the "
"schema for your data as well as tracking data like what the new type is "
"called. You can then create instances of your custom objects which your "
"[controllers](../cronjob-tutorial/controller-overview.md) manage."
msgstr ""

#: src/cronjob-tutorial/gvks.md:60
msgid ""
"Your APIs and resources represent your solutions on the clusters. Basically, "
"the CRDs are a definition of your customized Objects, and the CRs are an "
"instance of it."
msgstr ""

#: src/cronjob-tutorial/gvks.md:62
msgid "Ah, do you have an example?"
msgstr ""

#: src/cronjob-tutorial/gvks.md:64
msgid ""
"Think about the classic scenario where the goal is to have an application "
"and its database running on the platform with Kubernetes. Then, one CRD "
"could represent the App, and another one could represent the DB. By having "
"one CRD to describe the App and another one for the DB, you avoid hurting "
"concepts such as encapsulation, the single responsibility principle, and "
"cohesion. Damaging these concepts could cause unexpected side effects, such "
"as difficulty in extending, reuse, or maintenance, just to mention a few."
msgstr ""

#: src/cronjob-tutorial/gvks.md:66
msgid ""
"In this way, you can create the App CRD which has its controller and which "
"would be responsible for things like creating Deployments that contain the "
"App and creating Services to access it and etc. Similarly, you could create "
"a CRD to represent the DB, and deploy a controller that would manage DB "
"instances."
msgstr ""

#: src/cronjob-tutorial/gvks.md:68
msgid "Err, but what is that Scheme thing?"
msgstr ""

#: src/cronjob-tutorial/gvks.md:70
msgid ""
"The `Scheme` you saw before is simply a way to keep track of what Go type "
"corresponds to a given GVK (do not be overwhelmed by its "
"[godocs](https://pkg.go.dev/k8s.io/apimachinery/pkg/runtime?tab=doc#Scheme))."
msgstr ""

#: src/cronjob-tutorial/gvks.md:74
msgid ""
"For instance, suppose you mark the "
"`\"tutorial.kubebuilder.io/api/v1\".CronJob{}` type as being in the "
"`batch.tutorial.kubebuilder.io/v1` API group (implicitly saying it has the "
"Kind `CronJob`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/cronjob-tutorial/gvks.md:79
msgid ""
"Then, you can later construct a new `&CronJob{}` given some JSON from the "
"API server that says"
msgstr ""

