#: src/cronjob-tutorial/api-design.md:3
msgid ""
"In Kubernetes, there are a few rules for how you design APIs. Namely, all "
"serialized fields _must_ be `camelCase`, so use JSON struct tags to specify "
"this.  You can also use the `omitempty` struct tag to mark that a field "
"should be omitted from serialization when empty."
msgstr ""

#: src/cronjob-tutorial/api-design.md:8
msgid ""
"Fields may use most of the primitive types.  Numbers are the exception: for "
"API compatibility purposes, accept three forms of numbers: `int32` and "
"`int64` for integers, and `resource.Quantity` for decimals."
msgstr ""

#: src/cronjob-tutorial/api-design.md:14
msgid ""
"Quantities are a special notation for decimal numbers that have an "
"explicitly fixed representation that makes them more portable across "
"machines.  You've probably noticed them when specifying resources requests "
"and limits on pods in Kubernetes."
msgstr ""

#: src/cronjob-tutorial/api-design.md:19
msgid ""
"They conceptually work similar to floating point numbers: they have a "
"significant, base, and exponent. Their serializable and human readable "
"format uses whole numbers and suffixes to specify values much the way you "
"would describe computer storage."
msgstr ""

#: src/cronjob-tutorial/api-design.md:24
msgid ""
"For instance, the value `2m` means `0.002` in decimal notation.  `2Ki` means "
"`2048` in decimal, while `2K` means `2000` in decimal.  To specify "
"fractions, switch to a suffix that lets you use a whole number: `2.5` is "
"`2500m`."
msgstr ""

#: src/cronjob-tutorial/api-design.md:29
msgid ""
"There are two supported bases: 10 and 2 (called decimal and binary, "
"respectively).  Decimal base is indicated with \"normal\" SI suffixes (e.g. "
"`M` and `K`), while Binary base is specified in \"mebi\" notation (e.g. `Mi` "
"and `Ki`).  Think [megabytes vs "
"mebibytes](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Binary_prefix)."
msgstr ""

#: src/cronjob-tutorial/api-design.md:37
msgid ""
"There is one other special type to use: `metav1.Time`.  This functions "
"identically to `time.Time`, except that it has a fixed, portable "
"serialization format."
msgstr ""

