#: src/reference/watching-resources.md:1
msgid "Watching resources"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/watching-resources.md:3
msgid ""
"When extending the Kubernetes API, ensure that your solutions behave "
"consistently with Kubernetes itself. For example, consider a `Deployment` "
"resource, which a controller manages. This controller is responsible for "
"responding to changes in the cluster—such as when you create, update, or "
"delete a `Deployment`—by triggering reconciliation to ensure the resource’s "
"state matches the desired state."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/watching-resources.md:8
msgid ""
"Similarly, when developing controllers, watch for relevant changes in "
"resources that are crucial to your solution. These changes—whether "
"creations, updates, or deletions—should trigger the reconciliation loop to "
"take appropriate actions and maintain consistency across the cluster."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/watching-resources.md:12
msgid ""
"The "
"[controller-runtime](https://github.com/kubernetes-sigs/controller-runtime) "
"library provides several ways to watch and manage resources."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/watching-resources.md:14
msgid "Primary resources"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/watching-resources.md:16
msgid ""
"The **Primary Resource** is the resource that your controller is responsible "
"for managing. For example, if you create a custom resource definition (CRD) "
"for `MyApp`, the corresponding controller is responsible for managing "
"instances of `MyApp`."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/watching-resources.md:20
msgid ""
"In this case, `MyApp` is the **Primary Resource** for that controller, and "
"your controller’s reconciliation loop focuses on ensuring the desired state "
"of these primary resources is maintained."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/watching-resources.md:23
msgid ""
"When you create a new API using Kubebuilder, the CLI scaffolds the following "
"default code, ensuring that the controller watches all relevant events—such "
"as creations, updates, and deletions—for (`For()`) the new API."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/watching-resources.md:27
msgid ""
"This setup guarantees that the system triggers the reconciliation loop "
"whenever you create, update, or delete an instance of the API:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/watching-resources.md:31
msgid ""
"// Watches the primary resource (e.g., MyApp) for create, update, delete "
"events\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/watching-resources.md:39
msgid "Secondary resources"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/watching-resources.md:41
msgid ""
"Your controller will likely also need to manage **Secondary Resources**, "
"which are the resources required on the cluster to support the **Primary "
"Resource**."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/watching-resources.md:44
msgid ""
"Changes to these **Secondary Resources** can directly impact the **Primary "
"Resource**, so the controller must watch and reconcile these resources "
"accordingly."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/watching-resources.md:47
msgid "Which are owned by the controller"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/watching-resources.md:49
msgid ""
"These **Secondary Resources**, such as `Services`, `ConfigMaps`, or "
"`Deployments`, when `Owned` by the controllers, the specific controller "
"creates and manages them and ties them to the **Primary Resource** via "
"[OwnerReferences](https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/overview/working-with-objects/owners-dependents/)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/watching-resources.md:53
msgid ""
"For example, if you have a controller to manage your CR(s) of the Kind "
"`MyApp` on the cluster, which represents your application solution, all "
"resources required to ensure that `MyApp` is up and running with the desired "
"number of instances are **Secondary Resources**. The code responsible for "
"creating, deleting, and updating these resources is part of the `MyApp` "
"Controller. Add the appropriate "
"[OwnerReferences](https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/overview/working-with-objects/owners-dependents/) "
"using the "
"[controllerutil.SetControllerReference](https://pkg.go.dev/sigs.k8s.io/controller-runtime/pkg/controller/controllerutil#SetOwnerReference) "
"function to indicate that these resources are owned by the same controller "
"responsible for managing `MyApp` instances, which the `MyAppReconciler` "
"reconciles."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/watching-resources.md:63
msgid ""
"Additionally, if you delete the **Primary Resource**, Kubernetes' garbage "
"collection mechanism ensures that all associated **Secondary Resources** are "
"automatically deleted in a cascading manner."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/watching-resources.md:67
msgid "Which are NOT `owned` by the controller"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/watching-resources.md:69
msgid ""
"Note that **Secondary Resources** can either be APIs/CRDs defined in your "
"project or in other projects that are relevant to the **Primary Resources**, "
"but which the specific controller is not responsible for creating or "
"managing."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/watching-resources.md:72
msgid ""
"For example, if you have a CRD that represents a backup solution (i.e. "
"`MyBackup`) for your `MyApp`, it might need to watch changes in the `MyApp` "
"resource to trigger reconciliation in `MyBackup` to ensure the desired "
"state. Similarly, `MyApp`'s behavior might also be impacted by CRDs/APIs "
"defined in other projects."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/watching-resources.md:77
msgid ""
"In both scenarios, these resources are treated as **Secondary Resources**, "
"even if they are not `Owned` (i.e., not created or managed) by the "
"`MyAppController`."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/watching-resources.md:80
msgid ""
"In Kubebuilder, resources that are not defined in the project itself and are "
"not a **Core Type** (those not defined in the Kubernetes API) are called "
"**External Types**."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/watching-resources.md:83
msgid ""
"An **External Type** refers to a resource that is not defined in your "
"project but one that you need to watch and respond to. For example, if "
"**Operator A** manages a `MyApp` CRD for application deployment, and "
"**Operator B** handles backups, **Operator B** can watch the `MyApp` CRD as "
"an external type to trigger backup operations based on changes in `MyApp`."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/watching-resources.md:89
msgid ""
"In this scenario, **Operator B** could define a `BackupConfig` CRD that "
"relies on the state of `MyApp`. By treating `MyApp` as a **Secondary "
"Resource**, **Operator B** can watch and reconcile changes in **Operator "
"A**'s `MyApp`, ensuring that the controller initiates backup processes "
"whenever you update or scale `MyApp`."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/watching-resources.md:93
msgid "General concept of watching resources"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/watching-resources.md:95
msgid ""
"Whether you define a resource within your project or it comes from an "
"external project, the concept of **Primary** and **Secondary Resources** "
"remains the same:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/watching-resources.md:97
msgid ""
"The **Primary Resource** is the resource the controller is primarily "
"responsible for managing."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/watching-resources.md:98
msgid ""
"**Secondary Resources** are those that you need to ensure the primary "
"resource works as desired."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/watching-resources.md:100
msgid ""
"Therefore, regardless of whether your project or another project defined the "
"resource, your controller can watch, reconcile, and manage changes to these "
"resources as needed."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/watching-resources.md:103
msgid "Why does watching the secondary resources matter?"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/watching-resources.md:105
msgid ""
"When building a Kubernetes controller, it is crucial to not only focus on "
"**Primary Resources** but also to monitor **Secondary Resources**. Failing "
"to track these resources can lead to inconsistencies in your controller's "
"behavior and the overall cluster state."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/watching-resources.md:110
msgid ""
"Secondary resources may not be directly managed by your controller, but "
"changes to these resources can still significantly impact the primary "
"resource and your controller's functionality. Here are the key reasons why "
"it is important to watch them:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/watching-resources.md:115
msgid "**Ensuring Consistency**:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/watching-resources.md:116
msgid ""
"Secondary resources (e.g., child objects or external dependencies) may "
"diverge from their desired state. For instance, a secondary resource may be "
"modified or deleted, causing the system to fall out of sync."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/watching-resources.md:118
msgid ""
"Watching secondary resources ensures that any changes are detected "
"immediately, allowing the controller to reconcile and restore the desired "
"state."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/watching-resources.md:121
msgid "**Avoiding Random Self-Healing**:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/watching-resources.md:122
msgid ""
"Without watching secondary resources, the controller may \"heal\" itself "
"only upon restart or when specific events trigger. This can cause "
"unpredictable or delayed reactions to issues."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/watching-resources.md:124
msgid ""
"Monitoring secondary resources ensures that inconsistencies are addressed "
"promptly, rather than waiting for a controller restart or external event to "
"trigger reconciliation."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/watching-resources.md:127
msgid "**Effective Lifecycle Management**:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/watching-resources.md:128
msgid ""
"Secondary resources might not be owned by the controller directly, but their "
"state still impacts the behavior of primary resources. Without watching "
"these, you risk leaving orphaned or outdated resources."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/watching-resources.md:130
msgid ""
"Watching non-owned secondary resources lets the controller respond to "
"lifecycle events (create, update, delete) that might affect the primary "
"resource, ensuring consistent behavior across the system."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/watching-resources.md:133
msgid ""
"See [Watching Secondary Resources That Are Not "
"Owned](./watching-resources/secondary-resources-not-owned.md#configuration-example) "
"for an example."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/watching-resources.md:135
msgid ""
"Why not use `RequeueAfter X` for all scenarios instead of watching resources?"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/watching-resources.md:137
msgid ""
"Kubernetes controllers are fundamentally **event-driven**. When creating a "
"controller, the **Reconciliation Loop** is typically triggered by **events** "
"such as `create`, `update`, or `delete` actions on resources. This "
"event-driven approach is more efficient and responsive compared to "
"constantly requeuing or polling resources using `RequeueAfter`. This ensures "
"that the system only takes action when necessary, maintaining both "
"performance and efficiency."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/watching-resources.md:143
msgid ""
"In many cases, **watching resources** is the preferred approach for ensuring "
"Kubernetes resources remain in the desired state. It is more efficient, "
"responsive, and aligns with Kubernetes' event-driven architecture. However, "
"there are scenarios where `RequeueAfter` is appropriate and necessary, "
"particularly for managing external systems that do not emit events or for "
"handling resources that take time to converge, such as long-running "
"processes. Relying solely on `RequeueAfter` for all scenarios can lead to "
"unnecessary overhead and delayed reactions. Therefore, it is essential to "
"prioritize **event-driven reconciliation** by configuring your controller to "
"**watch resources** whenever possible, and reserving `RequeueAfter` for "
"situations where you need periodic checks."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/watching-resources.md:152
msgid "When `RequeueAfter X` is useful"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/watching-resources.md:154
msgid ""
"While `RequeueAfter` is not the primary method for triggering "
"reconciliations, there are specific cases where it is necessary, such as:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/watching-resources.md:157
msgid ""
"**Observing External Systems**: When working with external resources that do "
"not generate events (e.g., external databases or third-party services), "
"`RequeueAfter` allows the controller to periodically check the status of "
"these resources."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/watching-resources.md:160
msgid ""
"**Time-Based Operations**: Some tasks, such as rotating secrets or renewing "
"certificates, must happen at specific intervals. `RequeueAfter` ensures "
"these operations are performed on schedule, even when no other changes occur."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/watching-resources.md:163
msgid ""
"**Handling Errors or Delays**: When managing resources that encounter errors "
"or require time to self-heal, `RequeueAfter` ensures the controller waits "
"for a specified duration before checking the resource’s status again, "
"avoiding constant reconciliation attempts."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/watching-resources.md:167
msgid "Usage of predicates"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/watching-resources.md:169
msgid ""
"For more complex use cases, "
"[Predicates](https://pkg.go.dev/sigs.k8s.io/controller-runtime/pkg/predicate) "
"can be used to fine-tune when your controller should trigger reconciliation. "
"Predicates allow you to filter events based on specific conditions, such as "
"changes to particular fields, labels, or annotations, ensuring that your "
"controller only responds to relevant events and operates efficiently."
msgstr ""

