#: src/reference/controller-gen.md:165
msgid ""
"Each path is classified as either a filesystem path or a Go package/module "
"name, following the same rules used by the \"go\" command. A path is treated "
"as a filesystem path if it is absolute, or begins with \".\" or \"..\". All "
"other paths are treated as Go package or module names. For more information "
"on path classification, run \"go help packages\"."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/controller-gen.md:173
msgid ""
"    // brace-delimited form (preferred when specifying multiple paths)\n"
"    paths=&#34;{./api/..., ./pkg/...}&#34;\n"
"    \n"
"    // semicolon-separated form\n"
"    paths=./api/...;./pkg/...\n"
"    \n"
"    // filesystem paths (absolute or beginning with . or ..)\n"
"    paths=./...\n"
"    paths=./api/v1\n"
"    paths=/home/user/project/api\n"
"    \n"
"    // Go package or module names\n"
"    paths=sigs.k8s.io/controller-tools/pkg/...\n"
"    paths=example.com/myproject/api/v1\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/controller-gen.md:188
msgid ""
"When a filesystem path ends with \"...\", controller-gen crosses module "
"boundaries and includes packages from any nested Go modules found in "
"subdirectories (directories that contain their own go.mod file). This is "
"useful when working in a repository with multiple Go modules and you want to "
"run generation across all of them in a single invocation. This behavior does "
"not apply to Go package or module names, which follow standard Go module "
"rules and stay within a single module."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/controller-gen.md:198
msgid ""
"    // given this layout:\n"
"    //   myrepo/go.mod\n"
"    //   myrepo/api/v1/\n"
"    //   myrepo/subproject/go.mod  (nested module)\n"
"    //   myrepo/subproject/api/\n"
"    \n"
"    // this includes packages from both modules:\n"
"    paths=./...\n"
"    \n"
"    // this includes only the root module:\n"
"    paths=sigs.k8s.io/myrepo/...\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/controller-gen.md:210
msgid ""
"To limit generation to a single module, use a Go package or module name "
"rather than a filesystem path."
msgstr ""

