#: src/reference/pprof-tutorial.md:1
msgid "Monitoring performance with Pprof"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/pprof-tutorial.md:3
msgid ""
"[Pprof](https://github.com/google/pprof), a Go profiling tool, helps "
"identify performance bottlenecks in areas like CPU and memory usage. It's "
"integrated with the controller-runtime library's HTTP server, enabling "
"profiling via HTTP endpoints. You can visualize the data using go tool "
"pprof. Since [Pprof](https://github.com/google/pprof) is built into "
"controller-runtime, no separate installation is needed. [Manager "
"options](https://pkg.go.dev/sigs.k8s.io/controller-runtime/pkg/manager) make "
"it easy to enable pprof and gather runtime metrics to optimize controller "
"performance."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/pprof-tutorial.md:8
msgid ""
"While [Pprof](https://github.com/google/pprof) is an excellent tool for "
"profiling and debugging, it is not recommended to leave it enabled in "
"production environments. The primary reasons are:"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/pprof-tutorial.md:10
msgid ""
"**Security Risk**: The profiling endpoints expose detailed information about "
"your application's performance and resource usage, which could be exploited "
"if accessed by unauthorized users."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/pprof-tutorial.md:11
msgid ""
"**Overhead**: Running profiling can introduce performance overhead, mainly "
"CPU usage, especially under heavy load, potentially impacting production "
"workloads."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/pprof-tutorial.md:15
msgid "How to use Pprof"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/pprof-tutorial.md:17
